SVM Summary:

SVC: Used for classification. It can handle linear and non-linear data using kernels.
LinearSVC: Used for classification when data is linearly separable. It is faster but only creates a straight decision boundary.
Kernels: Methods that implicitly transform non-linear data into a higher-dimensional space so SVM can find a linear boundary there.
When to use transformation:
With SVC, kernels automatically handle the transformation.
With LinearSVC, you must manually transform features if the data is not linearly separable.

SVM Summary:
SVC: Used for classification. It handles linear and non-linear data using kernels.
LinearSVC: Used for linear data only. It is faster and creates a straight decision boundary.
Kernels: Methods that implicitly transform non-linear data into a higher-dimensional space so SVM can separate it with a linear boundary.
When to use kernels:
If data points are overlapped and cannot be separated by a straight line, use SVC with kernels.
The kernel creates a higher-dimensional representation where separation becomes easier and reduces misclassified points.
Example:
Data is non-linear in 2D → cannot separate with a straight line.
Use SVC with RBF kernel → it considers the data in a higher dimension (like 3D or more) → finds a better separating boundary.
Types of kernels:
Polynomial kernel
RBF (Gaussian) kernel
Sigmoid kernel
One-line difference:

SVC + kernel → handles complex/non-linear boundaries.
LinearSVC → fast, only for simple linear boundaries.